<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Product shell for dist/wds-win-x86_64.msi.
  File components are harvested at packaging time via wixl-heat, then
  scripts/win-msi-harvest.py pins wds_editor.exe + private DLLs under INSTALLDIR.
  Config is NOT installed: runtime writes %LOCALAPPDATA%\WDS\config\config.yml.
  UI: scripts/win-msi-ui.wxs (install dir + shortcuts + progress + exit).

  Language must stay 1033 to match wixl Template summary (x64;1033).

  dest rebuilds are the SAME product (fixed ProductCode, ProductVersion 1.0.0),
  so a second MSI is a maintenance install, not a major upgrade: costing keeps
  the existing INSTALLDIR and CreateShortcuts runs against that folder, and
  ARP's repair/in-place reinstall keeps working. MajorUpgrade exists only to
  remove leftover Id='*' packages from older dest builds.

  Because ProductVersion is pinned, ordering has to come from somewhere else:
  the numeric build stamp. InstallStamp writes WDS_BUILD_TS (seconds since
  2020-01-01Z, i.e. `date -u +%s - 1577836800`) to
  HKLM\Software\WDS\Editor\BuildTs, and AppSearch reads it back into
  WDS_INSTALLED_TS. It must stay inside the signed 32-bit range msiexec compares
  in, which is the only reason it is not a raw Unix timestamp. A companion
  BuildId string holds <sha>-<UTC time> for diagnostics; no condition reads it.

  Three outcomes, all decided before costing:
    - WDS_INSTALLED_TS =  WDS_BUILD_TS -> the SAME MSI again: REPAIR
      (REINSTALL=ALL + REINSTALLMODE=ecmus, the stock repair mode: missing,
      corrupt or equal-version files, registry and shortcuts).
    - WDS_INSTALLED_TS <> WDS_BUILD_TS -> a DIFFERENT build: UPDATE in place
      (REINSTALL=ALL + REINSTALLMODE=vamus, forcing every file because a
      same-version rebuild is invisible to version and checksum rules, and
      re-caching the package).
    - WDS_INSTALLED_TS >  WDS_BUILD_TS -> that build is NEWER: the
      LaunchCondition refuses the install. That is same-version downgrade
      protection; WDS_ALLOW_DOWNGRADE=1 overrides it.
  msiexec exposes no property for the installed package code, which is why a
  stamp of our own is needed at all.

  ProductCode and the component GUIDs are a MATCHED PAIR - never change one
  without the other:
    - This fixed ProductCode requires PATH-STABLE component GUIDs, so a later
      build's components are the same components and refcounts keep the files.
      win-msi-harvest.py derives them with UUID5 from the install-relative
      path, and package-target.sh rejects Guid='*' on the payload component.
    - A per-build ProductCode (Product Id='*') would instead need per-build
      unique component GUIDs. Mixing a fresh ProductCode with these shared
      GUIDs makes two different products claim the same component: costing
      treats it as already installed and skips InstallFiles, then the old
      product's uninstall deletes the files it skipped. That combination is
      what 224cdbd and 67c5dd8 were fighting; do not recreate it.

  RemoveExistingProducts sits immediately after InstallInitialize ("after
  InstallInitialize and before any actions that generate execution script",
  one of the four placements the action documents). It now only ever removes a
  leftover Id='*' product, whose component GUIDs are NOT ours, so that
  uninstall cannot delete files this build writes; the removal is also inside
  the transaction, so a later failure can roll it back. Never sequence it next
  to InstallFiles or InstallFinalize-after-file-actions: msiexec rejects the
  mid-script placement with error 2613.

  Shortcuts are MSI Shortcut table entries (Advertise=no + Target=exe).
  wixl defaults Advertise=yes; the Target path keeps them non-advertised.
  CREATE_* + Component.Condition chooses which .lnk files exist. wixl 0.103
  crashes on <Component><Condition> (added in 0.106); package-target.sh
  writes those Condition columns after wixl. AppSearch + ShortcutPrefs
  remember the last checkbox state.

  InstallDirDlg uses Edit (not PathEdit). PathEdit on a not-yet-created
  INSTALLDIR can clear the property; Edit plus InitWdsInstallDir keeps it.
  First-install WDS_INSTALLDIR is applied BEFORE CostInitialize. Updates
  never retarget the directory.

  Repair/update rewrite files (REINSTALLMODE ecmus/vamus) and msiexec stages
  rollback copies as <volume>\Config.Msi\*.rbf. The default ROOTDRIVE is the
  local volume with the most free space, often a data drive such as D:\ whose
  root ACL does not let SYSTEM set file security. Error 1926 / Error 5 on
  D:\Config.Msi\*.rbf is that failure. SetRootDrive pins ROOTDRIVE to
  WindowsVolume before CostInitialize so rollback stays on the OS volume.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/2006/wi">
  <Product Id="8BEDBB5B-25A7-5B4A-81EB-8FE35C6B0907"
           Name="WDS Editor"
           Language="1033"
           Version="$(var.ProductVersion)"
           Manufacturer="WDS"
           UpgradeCode="A7E3C2B1-9F4D-4E8A-9C6B-1D2E3F4A5B6C">
    <Package InstallerVersion="500"
             Compressed="yes"
             InstallScope="perMachine"
             Description="WDS Chart Editor (Windows x64)"
             Comments="Self-contained editor; preferences live under LocalAppData"/>

    <!-- Retires older dest MSIs that used Product Id='*': a different
         ProductCode at the same version 1.0.0, hence AllowSameVersionUpgrades.
         Same-product dest rebuilds are Installed=1 and never reach this. -->
    <MajorUpgrade DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of WDS Editor is already installed."
                  AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes"/>
    <!-- ProductVersion is pinned, so the MajorUpgrade guard above (which
         compares versions) can never fire between dest builds. Compare the
         build time instead: refuse when the installed build is newer. "&lt;="
         is escaped because this is XML text. -->
    <Condition Message="A newer build of WDS Editor is already installed (build [WDS_INSTALLED_ID]). Uninstall it first, or pass WDS_ALLOW_DOWNGRADE=1 to install this older build anyway.">NOT Installed OR NOT WDS_INSTALLED_TS OR WDS_INSTALLED_TS &lt;= WDS_BUILD_TS OR WDS_ALLOW_DOWNGRADE</Condition>
    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes"/>

    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="WdsEditorIcon"/>
    <!-- Overwrite mode for a normal install (no prior product, or a legacy
         Id='*' product that RemoveExistingProducts already removed): force every
         file, rewrite machine and user registry, reinstall shortcuts. The
         recache flag 'v' is deliberately NOT here - msiexec forbids it on a
         first installation - and the Installed-only actions below override this
         value: repair for the same build, vamus for a different build. -->
    <Property Id="REINSTALLMODE" Value="amus"/>
    <!-- Fresh install defaults to both shortcuts on; AppSearch overlays HKLM prefs. -->
    <Property Id="CREATE_DESKTOP_SHORTCUT" Value="1" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="FindDesktopPref"
                      Root="HKLM"
                      Key="Software\WDS\Editor"
                      Name="CreateDesktopShortcut"
                      Type="raw"
                      Win64="yes"/>
    </Property>
    <Property Id="CREATE_STARTMENU_SHORTCUT" Value="1" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="FindStartMenuPref"
                      Root="HKLM"
                      Key="Software\WDS\Editor"
                      Name="CreateStartMenuShortcut"
                      Type="raw"
                      Win64="yes"/>
    </Property>
    <!-- Parent folder chosen in BrowseDlg; CustomAction appends "\WDS Editor". -->
    <Property Id="WDSINSTALLPARENT" Secure="yes"/>
    <!-- Edit (not PathEdit) binds here. Copied to INSTALLDIR on first-install Next. -->
    <Property Id="WDS_INSTALLDIR" Secure="yes"/>
    <!-- Remembered path from an older Id='*' MSI (Win64 HKLM, 32-bit fallback).
         Same-product updates use the existing Directory table, not this. -->
    <Property Id="WDS_PREVIOUS_INSTALL" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="FindWdsInstallDir"
                      Root="HKLM"
                      Key="Software\WDS\Editor"
                      Name="InstallDir"
                      Type="raw"
                      Win64="yes"/>
    </Property>
    <Property Id="WDS_PREVIOUS_INSTALL32" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="FindWdsInstallDir32"
                      Root="HKLM"
                      Key="Software\WDS\Editor"
                      Name="InstallDir"
                      Type="raw"
                      Win64="no"/>
    </Property>
    <!-- Build stamp, injected at package time (wixl -D BuildTs=... -D BuildId=...).
         WDS_BUILD_TS is the one that decides: equal means the same MSI is being
         run again, smaller means the installed build is older, larger means it
         is newer. It counts seconds from 2020-01-01Z to stay inside the signed
         32-bit range msiexec compares in. Every package produced gets a new
         value, even from an unchanged commit, so only the very same .msi file
         can reuse one. WDS_BUILD_ID is diagnostics only. -->
    <Property Id="WDS_BUILD_TS" Value="$(var.BuildTs)" Secure="yes"/>
    <Property Id="WDS_BUILD_ID" Value="$(var.BuildId)" Secure="yes"/>
    <Property Id="WDS_INSTALLED_TS" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="FindWdsBuildTs"
                      Root="HKLM"
                      Key="Software\WDS\Editor"
                      Name="BuildTs"
                      Type="raw"
                      Win64="yes"/>
    </Property>
    <Property Id="WDS_INSTALLED_ID" Secure="yes">
      <RegistrySearch Id="FindWdsBuildId"
                      Root="HKLM"
                      Key="Software\WDS\Editor"
                      Name="BuildId"
                      Type="raw"
                      Win64="yes"/>
    </Property>
    <!-- Escape hatch for the downgrade guard, e.g. to go back to an older build
         while bisecting: msiexec /i pkg.msi WDS_ALLOW_DOWNGRADE=1 -->
    <Property Id="WDS_ALLOW_DOWNGRADE" Secure="yes"/>
    <CustomAction Id="SetInstallDirFromBrowse"
                  Property="INSTALLDIR"
                  Value="[WDSINSTALLPARENT]\WDS Editor"/>
    <CustomAction Id="AdoptPreviousInstall32"
                  Property="WDS_PREVIOUS_INSTALL"
                  Value="[WDS_PREVIOUS_INSTALL32]"/>
    <CustomAction Id="SetInstallDirFromPrevious"
                  Property="INSTALLDIR"
                  Value="[WDS_PREVIOUS_INSTALL]"/>
    <CustomAction Id="InitWdsInstallDir"
                  Property="WDS_INSTALLDIR"
                  Value="[INSTALLDIR]"/>
    <CustomAction Id="ApplyWdsInstallDir"
                  Property="INSTALLDIR"
                  Value="[WDS_INSTALLDIR]"/>
    <CustomAction Id="SetArpInstallLocation"
                  Property="ARPINSTALLLOCATION"
                  Value="[INSTALLDIR]"/>
    <!-- Must end with '\'. WindowsVolume is the OS volume (typically C:\).
         Command-line ROOTDRIVE= still wins (NOT ROOTDRIVE). -->
    <CustomAction Id="SetRootDrive"
                  Property="ROOTDRIVE"
                  Value="[WindowsVolume]"/>
    <CustomAction Id="SetReinstallAll"
                  Property="REINSTALL"
                  Value="ALL"/>
    <!-- Reinstalling over the SAME build: repair. ecmus is exactly what the
         stock maintenance screen's Repair button publishes (VerifyReadyDlg:
         ReinstallMode=ecmus), so the interactive and silent paths agree:
         missing, corrupt or equal-version files are rewritten plus registry and
         shortcuts, and the package is not re-cached. 'a' is deliberately absent
         - forcing every file is the update path's job. -->
    <CustomAction Id="SetReinstallModeRepair"
                  Property="REINSTALLMODE"
                  Value="ecmus"/>
    <!-- Reinstalling over a DIFFERENT build: update in place. Force every file
         ('a') because a same-version rebuild is invisible to version/checksum
         rules, and re-cache the package ('v') so a later Repair cannot restore
         the previous build out of %WINDIR%\Installer. -->
    <CustomAction Id="SetReinstallModeUpdate"
                  Property="REINSTALLMODE"
                  Value="vamus"/>

    <UIRef Id="WdsUI"/>

    <InstallUISequence>
      <Custom Action="AdoptPreviousInstall32" After="AppSearch">
        WDS_PREVIOUS_INSTALL32 AND NOT WDS_PREVIOUS_INSTALL AND NOT Installed
      </Custom>
      <Custom Action="SetInstallDirFromPrevious" After="AdoptPreviousInstall32">
        WDS_PREVIOUS_INSTALL AND NOT Installed
      </Custom>
      <!-- After costing so INSTALLDIR is the resolved path (existing product
           or remembered / default). Dialogs are After this action. -->
      <Custom Action="InitWdsInstallDir" After="CostFinalize">
        NOT WDS_INSTALLDIR
      </Custom>
      <Custom Action="SetRootDrive" After="AppSearch" Before="CostInitialize">
        NOT ROOTDRIVE
      </Custom>
    </InstallUISequence>
    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <!-- Only ever runs for a leftover Product Id='*' product (different
           ProductCode). FindRelatedProducts skips maintenance of this exact
           ProductCode, so a same-product dest rebuild never removes itself.
           Placement is window 2 - see the header; InstallFiles is 2613. -->
      <RemoveExistingProducts After="InstallInitialize"/>
      <Custom Action="AdoptPreviousInstall32" After="AppSearch">
        WDS_PREVIOUS_INSTALL32 AND NOT WDS_PREVIOUS_INSTALL AND NOT Installed
      </Custom>
      <Custom Action="SetInstallDirFromPrevious" After="AdoptPreviousInstall32">
        WDS_PREVIOUS_INSTALL AND NOT Installed
      </Custom>
      <!-- User-typed / UI path must be applied before costing, not after. -->
      <Custom Action="SetRootDrive" After="AppSearch" Before="CostInitialize">
        NOT ROOTDRIVE
      </Custom>
      <Custom Action="ApplyWdsInstallDir" After="SetInstallDirFromPrevious" Before="CostInitialize">
        WDS_INSTALLDIR AND NOT Installed
      </Custom>
      <Custom Action="SetReinstallAll" After="AppSearch" Before="CostInitialize">
        Installed AND NOT REMOVE AND NOT REINSTALL AND NOT RESUME AND NOT PATCH
      </Custom>
      <!-- Same MSI already installed: WDS_INSTALLED_TS (read by AppSearch)
           equals this package's stamp, so repair instead of overwriting. A
           leftover Id='*' product outranks repair and falls through to update,
           matching the MaintenanceTypeDlg / UpdateDlg split in win-msi-ui.wxs.
           NOT REMOVE keeps this out of the maintenance screen's Remove path. -->
      <Custom Action="SetReinstallModeRepair" After="AppSearch" Before="CostInitialize">
        Installed AND NOT REMOVE AND NOT RESUME AND NOT PATCH AND WDS_INSTALLED_TS = WDS_BUILD_TS AND NOT WIX_UPGRADE_DETECTED AND NOT WIX_SAME_VERSION_UPGRADE_DETECTED
      </Custom>
      <!-- An older build, or one that predates the stamp (the property is empty
           then): update in place. No NOT REINSTALL here on purpose - the UI may
           have set REINSTALL already and the mode still has to be normalised
           for this case. A NEWER installed build never reaches this: the
           LaunchCondition above refuses the install first. "&lt;>" is
           msiexec's not-equal operator, escaped because this is XML text. -->
      <Custom Action="SetReinstallModeUpdate" After="AppSearch" Before="CostInitialize">
        Installed AND NOT REMOVE AND NOT RESUME AND NOT PATCH AND (NOT WDS_INSTALLED_TS OR WDS_INSTALLED_TS &lt;> WDS_BUILD_TS)
      </Custom>
      <Custom Action="SetArpInstallLocation" After="InstallValidate">
        NOT REMOVE="ALL"
      </Custom>
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

    <Directory Id="TARGETDIR" Name="SourceDir">
      <Directory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
        <Directory Id="INSTALLDIR" Name="WDS Editor"/>
      </Directory>
      <Directory Id="ProgramMenuFolder">
        <Directory Id="WdsStartMenuFolder" Name="WDS Editor"/>
      </Directory>
      <Directory Id="DesktopFolder" Name="Desktop"/>
    </Directory>

    <DirectoryRef Id="INSTALLDIR">
      <Component Id="RegistryInstallDir" Guid="E0F1A2B3-4C5D-6E7F-8091-A2B3C4D5E6F7" Win64="yes">
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                       Key="Software\WDS\Editor"
                       Name="InstallDir"
                       Type="string"
                       Value="[INSTALLDIR]"
                       KeyPath="yes"/>
      </Component>
      <!-- MSI-owned prefs (WriteRegistryValues). AppSearch reads these back
           so ShortcutsDlg defaults match the last choice. -->
      <Component Id="ShortcutPrefs" Guid="A91B2C3D-4E5F-6780-91A2-B3C4D5E6F708" Win64="yes">
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                       Key="Software\WDS\Editor"
                       Name="CreateDesktopShortcut"
                       Type="string"
                       Value="[CREATE_DESKTOP_SHORTCUT]"
                       KeyPath="yes"/>
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                       Key="Software\WDS\Editor"
                       Name="CreateStartMenuShortcut"
                       Type="string"
                       Value="[CREATE_STARTMENU_SHORTCUT]"/>
      </Component>
      <!-- Build identity of the installation: BuildTs decides repair / update /
           refuse, BuildId is there so a human can see which commit is
           installed. Stable GUID on purpose, see the header. -->
      <Component Id="InstallStamp" Guid="B1C2D3E4-5F60-4712-8394-A5B6C7D8E9F0" Win64="yes">
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                       Key="Software\WDS\Editor"
                       Name="BuildTs"
                       Type="string"
                       Value="[WDS_BUILD_TS]"
                       KeyPath="yes"/>
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                       Key="Software\WDS\Editor"
                       Name="BuildId"
                       Type="string"
                       Value="[WDS_BUILD_ID]"/>
      </Component>
    </DirectoryRef>

    <!-- Do not author <Condition> here: wixl 0.103 dumps core
         ("unhandled child Component node Condition"). The Condition
         column is set after compile: CREATE_DESKTOP_SHORTCUT="1". -->
    <DirectoryRef Id="DesktopFolder">
      <Component Id="DesktopShortcut" Guid="B20DDB70-DD5E-50A4-ABF8-F068021441F6" Win64="yes">
        <Shortcut Id="WdsEditorDesktop"
                  Directory="DesktopFolder"
                  Name="WDS Editor"
                  Description="World Dai Star chart editor"
                  Target="[#WdsFile_wds_editor_exe]"
                  WorkingDirectory="INSTALLDIR"
                  Icon="WdsEditorIcon"
                  Advertise="no"/>
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                       Key="Software\WDS\Editor"
                       Name="DesktopShortcutInstalled"
                       Type="string"
                       Value="1"
                       KeyPath="yes"/>
      </Component>
    </DirectoryRef>

    <!-- Condition column patched after wixl: CREATE_STARTMENU_SHORTCUT="1". -->
    <DirectoryRef Id="WdsStartMenuFolder">
      <Component Id="StartMenuShortcut" Guid="0E08832A-9A77-5EE6-9097-E9871258C748" Win64="yes">
        <Shortcut Id="WdsEditorStartMenu"
                  Directory="WdsStartMenuFolder"
                  Name="WDS Editor"
                  Description="World Dai Star chart editor"
                  Target="[#WdsFile_wds_editor_exe]"
                  WorkingDirectory="INSTALLDIR"
                  Icon="WdsEditorIcon"
                  Advertise="no"/>
        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveWdsStartMenuFolder" On="uninstall"/>
        <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                       Key="Software\WDS\Editor"
                       Name="StartMenuShortcutInstalled"
                       Type="string"
                       Value="1"
                       KeyPath="yes"/>
      </Component>
    </DirectoryRef>

    <!-- Prefer BMP-only ICO generated at package time (PNG-compressed ICO can crash msiexec UI). -->
    <Icon Id="WdsEditorIcon" SourceFile="$(var.SourceDir)/wds-msi.ico"/>

    <Feature Id="ProductFeature" Title="WDS Editor" Level="1" AllowAdvertise="no" Absent="disallow">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="ProductFiles"/>
      <ComponentRef Id="RegistryInstallDir"/>
      <ComponentRef Id="ShortcutPrefs"/>
      <ComponentRef Id="InstallStamp"/>
      <ComponentRef Id="DesktopShortcut"/>
      <ComponentRef Id="StartMenuShortcut"/>
    </Feature>
  </Product>
</Wix>
